From f95673fe463e8fea14f91fd4ecf3e7469ffb0779 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bartimoussmith-oss <bartimoussmith-oss@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 04:23:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] fix(harvest): make the Granicus harvester bounded,
 idempotent and publishable

Recurring Harvest (both repos run this file):
- No time budget: each run walked ~960 agendas and every attachment of two
  Granicus views and was cancelled by GitHub at the six-hour job limit
  (e.g. 43456506595: 15:49Z -> 21:50Z), twice a day. Runs now stop at a
  wall-clock budget and a new-document cap, wait between requests, and
  record progress so the next run resumes (harvest_progress).
- The publish step ran 'git add STORAGE_MANIFEST.md database/... || true'.
  STORAGE_MANIFEST.md is not tracked, so git add staged nothing and no
  harvest was ever committed. Files are now staged individually.
- 'git pull --rebase || true' ran on a dirty tree, was refused, and the
  later push was rejected whenever another bot had committed. Commit
  first, then rebase and push with retries.

Autonomous Granicus Harvester:
- Passed --committee/--date, which the script did not accept (exit 2), and
  did not install pdfplumber/pytesseract/pdf2image, imported at module
  level (ModuleNotFoundError). Options added; PDF/OCR libraries optional.
- Scheduled runs stamped every row with a fixed 2026-11-02.
- Inputs are passed through env instead of template substitution.

Harvester:
- Identical bytes under a second URL raised UNIQUE(attachment_sha256); the
  handler sat at agenda level, so the rest of that agenda was dropped.
  Inserts are ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING per document; the second URL is
  stored in document_aliases.
- A change to an already-archived document is logged once, not every run.
- Schema is ensured on start (fresh databases lacked
  robots_advisory_ignored and document_tamper_log).
- Robots behavior is unchanged (result recorded per row); --respect-robots
  is available.

tests/test_granicus_harvester.py: nine offline tests against a local fake
Granicus, including two consecutive runs on one database.
